  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe cold air surge is on schedule for late week as a reminder that \nwinter is on our doorstep, even during a strong El Nino year. The \ntemperature change follows a complex system of low pressure tracking \nthrough the Great Lakes and Ohio valley today and tonight. It brings \nseveral rounds of rain showers to the region until depth of below \nfreezing air becomes sufficient for snow Thursday night. Lake effect \ncontributions give the Thumb area the best chance for some \naccumulation by Friday morning. \n\nBands of rain showers continue increasing over SE Mi this afternoon \nand evening as the Ohio valley mid/upper level wave phases with the \nnorthern Ontario trough. These systems are well-defined in afternoon \nobservations, both in surface analysis and satellite imagery. Model \nanalysis fields indicate the leading low level frontal zone \nstretched out from the Ohio valley up into northern IN/OH, and this \nis where ongoing northern IL convection remains focused. The \ncombination of the mesoscale mid level circulation near southern \nLake Mi and the northern Ontario trough are building a mature 700 mb \nmoisture axis that feeds the shower pattern up to the northern Great \nLakes surface low and intermediate cold front entering northern \nLower Mi. This forecast cycle leans toward borderline categorical \ncoverage of light rain along and north of I-69 this evening and then \ndecreasing after midnight. The northern Ontario mid/upper level \ntrough then sharpens considerably overhead during Thursday, and this \npowers the primary cold front through Lower Mi during the afternoon. \nCoverage of rain showers becomes numerous to widespread again but is \nconfined relatively close to the surface trough as it moves north to \nsouth and exits into Ohio by Thursday evening. \n\nToday's forecast cycle has slightly higher QPF and a slightly larger \nfootprint of snow accumulation in favored areas of the Thumb Thursday \nnight, mainly near Saginaw Bay and the Port Huron vicinity. This is \nwhere wind speed and direction favor longer duration of more intense \nlake effect bands, but still subject to all of the limiting factors \nmentioned in our early morning forecast discussion. Lake Huron water \nin the 50s maintains an all rain boundary layer over the lake and at \nleast a rain/snow mix near the shoreline, then cooling to all snow \nfarther inland. The lake aggregate air mass that covers the rest of \nLower Mi is cold enough for snow, although REFS mean surface T and \nTd are still hovering around the mid 30s by sunrise Friday. These \nthermal characteristics continue to support holding snow ratio in \nthe single digits in borderline rain/snow mix for the event while \nmainly adopting the areal QPF adjustments from the 11/12Z model \ncycle. Considerations to also monitor on the side of accumulation \noverachievement are tied to model soundings that support an \nimpressive lake effect profile having convective depth around 10 kft \nwith large CAPE density into the DGZ. A resulting colder/high rate \ndominated thermal profile and surface temperature could be a \ncontributing factor in the much higher snow accumulation shown in \nREFS members and ensemble means through Friday morning. \n\nLobes of short wave energy round the west flank of the long wave \ntrough Friday afternoon into the weekend. This keeps Lake Huron \nactivated into the weekend while wind veers more offshore by \nSaturday. Extended range models indicate surface high pressure makes \nan aggressive move into Lower Mi for improving conditions by Sunday.",
